      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to seat 9 people around a round table:**\n   The number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people in a circle is \\((n-1)!\\). For 9 people, this is:\n   \\[\n   (9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Treat the four math majors as a single \"block\" or \"super\" person:**\n   If the four math majors sit in consecutive seats, we can think of them as a single unit. This reduces the problem to arranging 6 units (the \"block\" of 4 math majors and the 5 other people) around a round table. The number of ways to arrange 6 units in a circle is:\n   \\[\n   (6-1)! = 5! = 120\n   \\]\n   Within the \"block\" of 4 math majors, there are \\(4!\\) ways to arrange the 4 math majors among themselves. So, the total number of ways to arrange the 9 people such that the four math majors are consecutive is:\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability:**\n   The probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\\).",
